name w3-01-ew-radar-1
timeout 700
time 1991-01-17T00:00:00Z

# CAMPAIGN W3, SORTIE 1 OF 10 - DESERT STORM, THE FIRST NIGHTS: THE OPENING MOVE.
# THIS FILE IS THE HEAD OF THE CAMPAIGN'S CARRY CHAIN. Sortie 10 flies against the air defence this
# sortie leaves standing, and the one thing it can take away is the forward early-warning set. THE
# CHAIN CARRIES EXACTLY ONE CALLSIGN, DELIBERATELY: `karinod`. The second wave's aim point is
# `kariref`, declared in this file and in no other, so that nothing this sortie destroys can arrive in
# sortie 10 as an objective naming a unit the overlay has deleted. The Weasel, fighter and saturation
# sorties in between (02-09) are pairwise disjoint from both ends of the chain in EVERY unit they can
# lose, aircraft and ground, which is O5's rule and the reason the ground callsigns carry a per-sortie
# prefix everywhere except here.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/w3-desert-storm.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: none - it IS the control, of w3-02-net-intact.fbm.
#
# THE ANCHOR OF THIS SORTIE is the literal opening of the war: 02:38 local on 17 January 1991, eight
# AH-64 destroy two early-warning radar stations at treetop height, to blind the Iraqi IADS ("Kari")
# to the 1 300 sorties behind them [T4]. FlightBox has no helicopter (C7), no terrain to hug (C4) and
# no Hellfire (C8 is bombs and one anti-radiation round), so the act is flown by an F-16 with two
# Mk 84 in a level laydown. WHAT IS KEPT is the only thing the mission was about: an early-warning
# radar dies FIRST, and everything behind it flies through what is left.
#
# THE ARENA, and every coordinate in it is [SET] except where marked. Al-Tuwaitha nuclear research
# centre 33.20 N 44.52 E [T4, approximate]; Baghdad 33.31 N 44.36 E [T4]. FlightBox flies it under
# `--elev const` on a 0 m datum (the Baghdad plain is ~34 m) and with NO TERRAIN MASKING (gap C4).
# Scale at this latitude: 1 deg of longitude = 93 145 m, 1 deg of latitude = 111 132 m [DERIVED].
#   the forward EW set  `karinod`, a P-18, at 43.60000 - 86 km WEST of Tuwaitha, i.e. on the approach
#                       and 67 km outside the S-75's 30 km envelope. That separation is the anchor's
#                       own: the border radars were killed because they were reachable, and the
#                       Baghdad missile ring was not the same problem
#   the missile ring    S-75 at 44.32000, S-125 at 44.44000, ZSU-23-4 at 44.51000, all `emcon hold`
#   the target set      the Doura refinery `kariref` (33.10 44.42) and the Tuwaitha research centre
#                       `karitwa` (33.20 44.52), both named in the anchor [T4]. Only `kariref` is
#                       attacked here, and it exists in NO other file - which is what keeps the chain's
#                       carried callsign set to exactly one, `karinod`
#
# THE NET IS A BURIED CABLE, and that is sourced doctrine rather than convenience. `link wire` has no
# horizon and cannot be jammed (C24); a `link radio ... mast 12` between this node and the ring would
# have a 28.5 km radio horizon [DERIVED, 4.12*(sqrt(12)+sqrt(12))] against a 67 km separation, i.e. no
# link at all. Kari was a hardwired, centralised system, which is exactly why its radars had to be
# destroyed rather than talked over. MEASURED CONSEQUENCE, and it is this sortie's whole subject: with
# the cable intact the `emcon hold` batteries come up 8.0 s after the node sees the raid; with the node
# dead they never come up at all, because a bomber that keeps its own radar off gives their passive
# receivers nothing to hear.
#
# THE SUBSTITUTIONS, AND THEIR DIRECTION - the binding disclosure, repeated in every W3 file.
#   1. Apaches -> one F-16 with Mk 84: FlightBox loses the ingress profile and keeps the effect.
#   2. 100 mm AAA [T4] -> ZSU-23-4 (`zsu23`, 0-2 500 m). The campaign's AAA layer is therefore
#      SHORTER-RANGED than the anchor's and cannot reach a 5 000 m ingress at all. The defence is
#      WEAKER than history in that layer, and it is declared rather than hidden.
#   3. The SA-3 is the type the anchor confirms on at least one downed F-16 [T4]; it is here as `sa3`.
#
# NIGHT, AND WHAT IT IS WORTH HERE. `time 1991-01-17T00:00:00Z` = 03:00 local, sun elevation about
# -48 deg at the spawn point [DERIVED] - a real night by the daylight term's own threshold. IT BUYS
# NOTHING IN THIS TREE AND THE CAMPAIGN SAYS SO: nothing emits light (doc/sensors.md section 9), so a
# night mission's only measurable difference from a day one is that the visual channel is dead. No
# radar, no warning receiver, no missile seeker and no bomb changes by one bit. Every W3 result is
# therefore a result about RADAR AND RADIO, not about darkness.
#
# WHICH DEFENSIVE LAYER THIS FILE MEASURES: the MISSILE layer. There is no fighter in it. O5's rule
# holds here too - two layers cannot be measured in one file - and W3 splits its ten: 01/02 and 03/04/05
# measure the surface layer, 07/08/09 the fighter layer with the belt briefed quiet, 10 lets everything
# run at once and reports what was still airborne beside every number.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: IS ONE BOMB ON THE FORWARD EARLY-WARNING SET WORTH A SORTIE - i.e. does
# killing it change what the ring behind it does to the aircraft that follow? The second F-16 (`q1pen`)
# is the instrument: it flies the identical route in this file and in w3-02-net-intact.fbm, and the
# only thing that differs between the two runs is whether the node was alive when it crossed the ring.
#
# THE TWO ENGAGEMENTS ARE SEPARATED IN TIME, deliberately (O1's rule): the strike on the node happens at
# t ~ 105 s at 43.60 E, the crossing of the missile ring from t ~ 280 s at 44.0 E onward. A run ends at
# the FIRST flight-monitor K.O., so a mission that put both in the same 60 s would measure one of them.
#
# MEASURED (per-mission fingerprint 77df6f2fe18f1f2e, AS CAMPAIGN STEP 1; standalone is the same run,
# because step 1 inherits nothing):
#   the node          released t = 69.8, `stores DELIVERY` t = 104.7, `aimErrM` 43.3 m (long 40.8,
#                     across 14.7) against `predErrM` 61.8 m - the release computer's one-Cd table is
#                     the LARGER of the two errors, exactly as doc/missions/weapons.md says.
#                     `damage KILL unit=karinod` t = 104.7
#   the ring          3 `site RADIATE why="passive cue"` at t = 8.0 (the cable works), 6 `net CUE`,
#                     then at t = 108.0 three `net LOST ... reason=terminal` (distM 67.4 / 79.9 /
#                     84.8 km against reachM 277.8 km - the cable did not run out, the node died),
#                     `net AUTONOMOUS fallback=hold` x3 at t = 116.0, `site GO_DARK why="cue expired"`
#                     x3 at t = 200.0. ZERO `site TRACK`, ZERO `site LAUNCH`
#   the second wave   released t = 421.0, delivered t = 455.8, `aimErrM` 38.1 m, `kariref` DESTROYED,
#                     and it was never fired at
#   both aircraft     SUCCESS. Campaign carry out: `karinod` and `kariref` destroyed, 2 x mk84 expended
# AGAINST w3-02, WHICH IS THIS FILE WITH ONE TOKEN CHANGED: 10 `site LAUNCH`, 4 arrivals at 7.28-8.35 m,
# and the second wave's STORES SYSTEM FAILS at t = 392.4 so that it reaches its aim point at t = 448.3
# with nothing it can drop. ONE Mk 84 ON THE NODE IS WORTH THE WHOLE STRIKE.
#
# WHAT TO READ:
#   events.log      `net JOIN` / `net CUE` / `site RADIATE` counts, `site LAUNCH` count (0 here),
#                   `damage KILL unit=karinod`, `stores DELIVERY unit=q1pen_mk84_*`
#   telemetry_karisa3.csv  site_state, net_state, net_cue, site_launches
#
# READING RULE: exit 0 = SUCCESS is NOT the measurement. THE MEASUREMENT IS THE PAIR
# (`site LAUNCH` count, `UNIT_RESULT unit=kariref`) read beside w3-02-net-intact.fbm's, and the sortie
# is a failure of the MECHANISM only if the node survives the bomb.

net kari
  control karinod                               # the forward EW set: a search antenna and a telephone
  link wire                                     # Kari's buried cable: no horizon, and C24 cannot touch it
  period 4.0                                    # [SET] a ground reporting cycle, not a fighter's PPLI
  hold 3                                        # [SET] cycles of silence before a member is on its own
  wcs free
  member karinod
  member karisa2 autonomy hold                  # cut off from the node, these batteries wait in the dark
  member karisa3 autonomy hold
  member kariflk autonomy hold

unit q1str
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 33.20000 43.35000 5000 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set fcr_mode off                              # nothing on this jet radiates: the ring's ESM hears nothing
  set rwr on
  set store 3 mk84
  set store 7 mk84
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode ccrp
  wp 33.20000 43.60000 5000 450
  wp 33.20000 43.10000 5000 450                 # egress west, so this jet is never the ring's nearest track
  objective kill unit karinod
  objective survive

unit q1pen
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 33.10000 43.30000 5000 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 80
  set fcr_mode off
  set rwr on
  set cmds_mode auto
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set store 3 mk84
  set store 7 mk84
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  set attack_mode ccrp
  wp 33.10000 44.42000 5000 450
  wp 33.10000 43.90000 5000 450
  objective kill unit kariref
  objective survive

unit karinod
  module p18
  team hostile
  spawn 33.20000 43.60000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon free

unit karisa2
  module sa2
  team hostile
  spawn 33.14000 44.32000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon hold
  set rounds 6

unit karisa3
  module sa3
  team hostile
  spawn 33.06000 44.44000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon hold
  set rounds 4

unit kariflk
  module zsu23
  team hostile
  spawn 33.20000 44.51000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon hold
  set rounds 40

unit kariref
  module target_soft
  team hostile
  spawn 33.10000 44.42000 ground 0.0 0

unit karitwa
  module target_hard
  team hostile
  spawn 33.20000 44.52000 ground 0.0 0
